1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,880
The school 7F module is explicitly allocated this week. This session
follows each of its six narrative sections, but explains mechanisms
and decision links rather than copying the old companion’s
environmental-emergency catalogue.

2
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:25,656
A mechanism helps you predict what might happen next. It does not
identify the individual patient’s complete diagnosis, supply a missing
treatment order or authorize a procedure.

3
00:00:25,656 --> 00:00:36,264
For any disagreement, preserve three labels: what the school source
says, what current BCEHS guidance says, and what the actual licensed
provider is authorized to do.

4
00:00:36,264 --> 00:00:50,976
Thermal energy disrupts cell membranes and proteins. Tissue injury
triggers inflammation; when vascular permeability rises, fluid and
proteins move from the intravascular space into surrounding tissues.

5
00:00:50,976 --> 00:01:03,456
The result can include swelling at the burn site and reduced effective
circulating volume. Burn shock can combine vasodilation and capillary
leak; it is not explained by skin appearance alone.

6
00:01:03,456 --> 00:01:14,472
The time course and body area matter, but a single percentage does not
tell you current airway status, perfusion, depth, inhaled exposure or
other trauma.

7
00:01:14,472 --> 00:01:26,208
The school calls the early response the ebb phase. Circulating volume
and cardiac output may be reduced after a severe burn, limiting the
delivery of oxygen and nutrients.

8
00:01:26,208 --> 00:01:39,864
The later flow phase includes greater cardiac work and metabolic
demand. Stress hormones and inflammation can sustain protein breakdown
and increased energy needs long after the original exposure.

9
00:01:39,864 --> 00:01:54,336
The saved school text places the transition around forty-eight hours.
A published burn primer describes a longer initial period. These are
broad models, not a promise that every patient changes phase at one
fixed hour.

10
00:01:54,336 --> 00:02:07,992
For a field assessment, ask when the injury happened and what care has
occurred, then assess actual perfusion. Do not label new hypotension
as normal ebb physiology or assume later tachycardia proves infection.

11
00:02:07,992 --> 00:02:23,088
Edema can distort anatomy. In a circumferential limb burn, swelling
inside a relatively fixed skin boundary may reduce distal perfusion;
in a circumferential chest burn, chest-wall expansion can be
mechanically limited.

12
00:02:23,088 --> 00:02:36,264
Inhaled hot gases, smoke and chemical irritants can injure upper or
lower airways. Symptoms may evolve, so an early normal voice or oxygen
reading does not guarantee a stable airway.

13
00:02:36,264 --> 00:02:49,224
Current BCEHS H10 emphasizes repeated airway-patency assessment,
supplemental oxygen where indicated, and prompt reassessment when
cough, voice change or soot suggests inhalation injury.

14
00:02:49,224 --> 00:03:02,280
Above the vocal cords, heat injury can produce swelling and
obstruction. New hoarseness, noisy inspiration or increasing effort
needs attention even before the oxygen display falls.

15
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:18,048
Below the vocal cords, smoke chemicals can injure the airway lining,
impair clearance and provoke bronchospasm. Cough and wheeze point to
different observations from a narrowed upper airway, but do not
identify one cause by themselves.

16
00:03:18,048 --> 00:03:33,096
Upper thermal and lower chemical injury are useful patterns, not
absolute boundaries. The exposure, including steam or unusual
chemicals, can change the pattern; an external facial burn does not
describe the whole airway.

17
00:03:33,096 --> 00:03:51,096
Keep reassessing voice, airway sounds, effort, ventilation and
circulation. Plan escalation before deterioration; advanced airway
intervention belongs to appropriately trained and authorized
clinicians, not to a decision based only on singed hair.

18
00:03:51,096 --> 00:04:06,240
Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin and impairs oxygen carriage. A
standard two-wavelength pulse oximeter cannot reliably distinguish
carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin, so the displayed saturation may
mislead.

19
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:18,312
A suspected combustion exposure is a history-and-scene problem as well
as a monitor problem. Consider whether more than one person is
affected and whether symptoms match the exposure.

20
00:04:18,312 --> 00:04:34,080
Current BCEHS J02 directs oxygen and consultation for suspected or
confirmed CO. CO-oximetry, when available, can add information, but a
device reading is not a universal mandatory screen or a stand-alone
rule-out.

21
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:48,120
Think of oxygen transport as a delivery route. Carbon monoxide
occupies hemoglobin binding sites and interferes with oxygen delivery
and release. The usual pulse-oximeter number may not reveal that
problem.

22
00:04:48,120 --> 00:05:01,944
Cyanide acts further along the route by inhibiting mitochondrial
energy production. Oxygen may reach a tissue that cannot use it
normally. Combustion exposure can involve both poisons, as well as
airway injury.

23
00:05:01,944 --> 00:05:16,344
Severe unexplained neurologic or circulatory decline after an enclosed
fire raises concern, but no single bedside finding proves cyanide
poisoning. A high lactate, if available, has other possible causes.

24
00:05:16,344 --> 00:05:36,912
BCEHS J03 requires CliniCall consultation for suspected cyanide
exposure. Its hydroxocobalamin monograph labels the antidote for CCP
use. Recognize the concern, support the patient and communicate it; do
not convert the school antidote mention into a PCP administration
order.

25
00:05:36,912 --> 00:05:49,872
Burn depth is not always obvious at first contact and can change in
appearance. Ask about the mechanism, time, clothing, cooling already
provided and possible inhalation exposure.

26
00:05:49,872 --> 00:06:02,280
Rules of Nines and the patient’s hand can help estimate the area of
partial-thickness or deeper burns. Do not count uncomplicated
superficial redness in the BCEHS TBSA estimate.

27
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:15,864
For children and irregular patterns, the appropriate chart and current
local destination guidance matter. The percentage is an estimate for
communication, not a precise measurement or automatic fluid formula.

28
00:06:15,864 --> 00:06:32,784
In this original practice case, a worker is already outside a smoke-
filled equipment room. You find a small forearm burn, headache and a
reassuring-looking saturation. Begin with scene safety and the primary
survey, not with wound photography.

29
00:06:32,784 --> 00:06:48,336
During reassessment the voice becomes rough and breathing effort
increases. Explain the change: the airway may be evolving
independently of the visible skin injury. Reassess ventilation and
prepare the appropriate support and escalation.

30
00:06:48,336 --> 00:07:03,600
The headache and exposure history also keep combustion toxicity on the
problem list. A normal-looking conventional saturation cannot remove
carbon monoxide from that list. Do not wait for a large skin burn or a
device diagnosis.

31
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:20,112
A useful pre-alert describes the enclosed-smoke exposure, the new
voice change, breathing trend, circulation, treatment and response.
State concern for inhalation injury and toxic exposure while
acknowledging what remains unconfirmed.

32
00:07:20,112 --> 00:07:32,760
The core temperature reflects heat produced by metabolism and muscle
work minus heat lost to the environment. Radiation, conduction,
convection and evaporation all contribute.

33
00:07:32,760 --> 00:07:47,976
Vasoconstriction and shivering can help conserve or produce heat.
Sweating and skin blood flow can dissipate heat, but these responses
have limits and depend on hydration, age, illness, clothing and air
movement.

34
00:07:47,976 --> 00:08:01,464
A hot or cold environment is not the only cause. Immobility, wet
clothing, intoxication, illness, medications or a long extrication can
defeat normal compensation.

35
00:08:01,464 --> 00:08:14,760
Sweat cools mainly when it evaporates. In humid air, a person may be
visibly soaked while losing heat poorly. The presence of sweat is
therefore not evidence that temperature control is working.

36
00:08:14,760 --> 00:08:27,672
A wet patient on a cold surface can lose heat by contact, moving air
and evaporation together. Covering the chest alone does not correct
heat loss through the ground or an exposed wet back.

37
00:08:27,672 --> 00:08:40,944
Radiation and convection are heat-exchange processes, not always
cooling processes. Direct sunlight or air hotter than the skin may add
heat. Ask which direction the temperature gradient points.

38
00:08:40,944 --> 00:08:57,960
Link each environmental observation to a practical control: safer
shelter, shade, suitable clothing removal, insulation or active
cooling. Then repeat the patient assessment; changing the environment
does not prove that organ dysfunction has resolved.

39
00:08:57,960 --> 00:09:11,304
Heat illness exists on a spectrum. Cramps and heat exhaustion can
progress; altered mental status or other central nervous system
dysfunction in an overheated patient raises concern for heat stroke.

40
00:09:11,304 --> 00:09:23,520
Classic heat illness and exertional heat illness have different
exposure contexts, but both can cause life-threatening organ injury.
Sweating does not safely exclude exertional heat stroke.

41
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:39,288
Current BCEHS I02 says begin cooling on scene where possible and
continue during transport, with the method chosen around patient
condition, movement safety and available resources. Do not treat
environmental heat stroke with antipyretics.

42
00:09:39,288 --> 00:09:54,840
A volunteer becomes confused during heavy work in a hot warehouse. The
person is sweating. A forehead device reads thirty-eight point four
degrees Celsius. Neither finding safely dismisses dangerous
environmental heat illness.

43
00:09:54,840 --> 00:10:08,232
A surface reading is not a reliable substitute for the core
measurement in the formal heat-stroke definition. Record the site and
method, but do not wait for the display to cross forty before
responding to the clinical threat.

44
00:10:08,232 --> 00:10:23,256
Start cooling and support airway, breathing and circulation while
checking for competing or concurrent problems such as low glucose,
infection or intoxication. Environmental heat and another emergency
can coexist.

45
00:10:23,256 --> 00:10:39,024
BCEHS I02 calls for prompt cooling rather than antipyretics for
environmental heat illness. Repeat mental status, breathing and
perfusion during treatment. Oral water alone is not an effective
substitute for full-body cooling.

46
00:10:39,024 --> 00:10:56,952
Heat exhaustion often includes weakness, dizziness, thirst and
sweating without marked central nervous system impairment. New
confusion, ataxia or reduced consciousness is a warning of more
dangerous illness. Do not wait to settle the label before starting
cooling.

47
00:10:56,952 --> 00:11:10,584
Hypothermia is a core temperature below 35 degrees Celsius, but
peripheral temperature readings can be inaccurate. Clinical stage and
context are often more useful than claiming an exact core number in
the field.

48
00:11:10,584 --> 00:11:24,168
Remove the person from ongoing cold exposure when safe, remove wet
clothing, insulate and handle gently. Avoid unnecessary standing or
rough movement in a markedly cold patient.

49
00:11:24,168 --> 00:11:38,784
Shivering may disappear as illness worsens, but its absence alone does
not stage the patient. Look for mental-status change, pulse and
breathing trends, trauma, intoxication and other treatable causes.

50
00:11:38,784 --> 00:11:51,576
Cooling slows many cellular processes. A reduced heart rate in a
deeply cold patient belongs in that context, but does not remove the
need to assess perfusion and other causes of deterioration.

51
00:11:51,576 --> 00:12:04,248
The Osborn, or J, wave is a deflection near the end of the QRS complex
that can occur with hypothermia. It is not unique to cold exposure and
cannot supply an exact core temperature.

52
00:12:04,248 --> 00:12:18,096
Clinical stage, environmental history and repeated findings matter
when a thermometer is unreliable. A patient found on a floor indoors
can be cold because another illness prevented movement or normal heat
production.

53
00:12:18,096 --> 00:12:34,320
Use gentle handling, prevent further heat loss and assess concurrent
problems. If arrest is suspected, follow the actual current
hypothermia and resuscitation pathway; do not invent a pulse-check
interval by combining conflicting source versions.

54
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:48,192
Drowning is a respiratory impairment process following submersion, not
a special disease caused by saltwater alone. Aspiration can injure the
alveolar surface and interfere with surfactant and normal expansion.

55
00:12:48,192 --> 00:13:01,656
Partly collapsed or fluid-filled regions receive less ventilation
while blood continues to flow through them. This creates a
ventilation-perfusion mismatch and shunt, so oxygenation can be
difficult.

56
00:13:01,656 --> 00:13:16,344
A person who seems better immediately after rescue may later develop
respiratory deterioration. Current BCEHS H12 emphasizes oxygenation,
aspiration and heat-loss prevention, and conveyance for assessment.

57
00:13:16,344 --> 00:13:28,560
Surfactant helps reduce surface tension so alveoli remain easier to
keep open. Damage to that lining, edema and collapse can reduce the
surface available for gas exchange.

58
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:41,160
If blood continues through a region receiving little or no fresh gas,
it remains poorly oxygenated. That is the reason the school connects
atelectasis, shunt and worsening hypoxemia.

59
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:57,624
The school diagram includes breath-holding, laryngospasm and
aspiration. Treat this as a possible mechanism chain, not a required
sequence in every drowning event. Do not wait for evidence of every
step before supporting breathing.

60
00:13:57,624 --> 00:14:14,208
Current BCEHS H12 uses the same care principles for fresh and salt
water. Oxygenation, ventilation when needed, thermal protection,
reassessment and appropriate conveyance matter more than memorizing
separate water-type algorithms.

61
00:14:14,208 --> 00:14:26,184
Dysbaric illness relates to changing ambient pressure. During ascent,
trapped gas expands; during a rapid pressure reduction, inert gas
dissolved in tissue can form bubbles.

62
00:14:26,184 --> 00:14:39,048
Pulmonary barotrauma can cause pneumothorax or arterial gas embolism.
Decompression illness can produce pain, sensory or motor changes,
breathing difficulty or altered awareness.

63
00:14:39,048 --> 00:14:53,472
The dive profile and timing are essential history. Do not assume that
every post-dive symptom is decompression sickness, and do not let a
benign-looking skin examination overrule neurologic or respiratory
deterioration.

64
00:14:53,472 --> 00:15:09,888
Imagine a free gas volume of one litre at two units of absolute
pressure. At one unit, the simple Boyle model predicts two litres if
temperature and gas amount stay constant. This is a teaching
calculation, not a lung-volume target.

65
00:15:09,888 --> 00:15:26,592
A lung cannot expand without limit. Trapped gas during ascent can
injure tissue, whereas decompression sickness involves gas that was
dissolved in tissues forming bubbles as pressure falls. The mechanisms
are related to pressure but are not identical.

66
00:15:26,592 --> 00:15:40,848
Decompression illness is the broader term covering decompression
sickness and arterial gas embolism. A reported normal ascent or
compliance with a dive computer does not exclude illness, and symptom
patterns may overlap.

67
00:15:40,848 --> 00:15:58,752
Record dive depth and duration, breathing gas, repeated dives, ascent
events and symptom times. New weakness, altered awareness or breathing
difficulty requires urgent assessment and specialist coordination,
even if symptoms improve after oxygen.

68
00:15:58,752 --> 00:16:12,528
A diver who is short of breath may have lung barotrauma as well as
other problems. Do not automatically borrow the CPAP approach used for
selected drowning patients: the mechanisms and contraindications
differ.

69
00:16:12,528 --> 00:16:30,096
Current BCEHS I03 directs the highest possible oxygen percentage and
flow for symptomatic patients, continuing even if symptoms resolve.
Support inadequate ventilation with the trained airway and bag-mask
pathway; assess breathing effectiveness repeatedly.

70
00:16:30,096 --> 00:16:44,664
I03 contraindicates CPAP and PEEP because underlying barotrauma may
worsen. Keep the patient supine where possible, changing position when
needed to protect the airway and respond to the clinical condition.

71
00:16:44,664 --> 00:16:58,008
Arrange emergency physician assessment and hyperbaric specialist
coordination through the service pathway. Do not independently select
a chamber destination or send a symptomatic diver back into the water.

72
00:16:58,008 --> 00:17:30,432
The linked physiology video introduces several different effects.
Nitrogen narcosis describes impaired function from breathing nitrogen
at raised ambient pressure; it is not the same mechanism as bubbles in
decompression sickness. Lung barotrauma can cause arterial gas
embolism. These explanations can guide questions, but overlapping
weakness, confusion or breathing difficulty after a dive requires
urgent assessment and the current service pathway, not a diagnosis
from one analogy.

73
00:17:30,432 --> 00:18:02,520
The video model does not establish fixed patient gas values. In
Boyle’s model, volume changes while the gas amount remains fixed.
Tissue uptake also depends on time. Carbon dioxide can accumulate if
elimination is inadequate; dense breathing gas, workload and
rebreathing are relevant despite the video’s reassuring statement.
Record the actual dive and symptom history and support breathing. A
chamber tour supplies neither a treatment table nor permission to
operate equipment.

74
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:12,960
At high altitude the fraction of oxygen in air remains about the same,
but barometric pressure is lower. The partial pressure driving oxygen
into blood therefore falls.

75
00:18:12,960 --> 00:18:25,224
Ventilation and circulation can adapt over time. Acclimatization is
not immediate, and a previously fit or experienced hiker can still
develop serious altitude illness.

76
00:18:25,224 --> 00:18:41,952
New ataxia or altered awareness suggests high-altitude cerebral edema;
dyspnea at rest raises concern for high-altitude pulmonary edema.
Current WMS guidance pairs urgent descent with oxygen when indicated
rather than delaying one for the other.

77
00:18:41,952 --> 00:18:54,168
Crush injury begins with compressive force and local ischemia. Muscle
cells lose membrane integrity and release intracellular material;
injured tissue also becomes edematous.

78
00:18:54,168 --> 00:19:09,528
When compression is relieved, reperfusion may deliver potassium,
myoglobin and other metabolites into the circulation. At the same
time, injured muscle can retain fluid, worsening effective
circulating-volume loss.

79
00:19:09,528 --> 00:19:21,240
The feared complications include dysrhythmia, acidosis, shock, kidney
injury and later organ dysfunction. A patient may look stable before
release and deteriorate afterward.

80
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:37,464
Muscle cells need ATP, their usable energy supply, to maintain ion
gradients. During prolonged ischemia, energy production falls and
membrane control becomes impaired. Direct compression can damage the
cell at the same time.

81
00:19:37,464 --> 00:19:52,056
Abnormal calcium handling activates damaging processes within the
cell. As muscle breaks down, intracellular potassium, myoglobin and
enzymes enter the surrounding fluid and may reach the circulation.

82
00:19:52,056 --> 00:20:09,600
Potassium affects cardiac electrical activity; myoglobin is associated
with kidney injury; creatine kinase is a marker of muscle damage.
These substances are not interchangeable, and a normal early ECG
cannot measure the total burden of muscle injury.

83
00:20:09,600 --> 00:20:24,960
Swollen muscle also sequesters fluid, and shock reduces renal
perfusion. Kidney injury is therefore not simply a pipe blocked by
pigment; several linked processes matter. Do not wait for dark urine
before recognizing the risk.

84
00:20:24,960 --> 00:20:40,752
Entrapment duration, involved muscle mass, compressive force, access,
temperature and associated trauma change the risk picture. Rescue work
also introduces structural, electrical, machinery and secondary-
collapse hazards.

85
00:20:40,752 --> 00:20:55,104
The current BCEHS H02 calls for coordinated planning and CliniCall
involvement for key pre-release treatment decisions. The lesson is to
prepare a time-stamped plan and reassessment rather than improvise a
fixed regimen.

86
00:20:55,104 --> 00:21:09,720
Do not assume that every trapped patient needs the same fluid volume,
that a tourniquet automatically prevents systemic effects, or that a
fast unplanned release is harmless. Apply the current guideline and
license-specific direction.

87
00:21:09,720 --> 00:21:28,080
For significant crush injury, current BCEHS H02 includes considering
two litres of normal saline immediately before release, with CliniCall
consultation recommended for fluid planning. This is a source-specific
consideration, not a fixed volume for every trapped limb.

88
00:21:28,080 --> 00:21:43,032
The same guideline requires consultation before salbutamol for this
indication. Calcium and other electrolyte treatments appear at
different provider levels. Do not import the whole treatment list into
an unsupervised PCP plan.

89
00:21:43,032 --> 00:22:00,576
The school narrative says even a small amount of lactated Ringer
solution can trigger immediate arrest in crush injury. That absolute
is not supported by the AAST rhabdomyolysis consensus, which accepts
either saline or lactated Ringer solution in its hospital context.

90
00:22:00,576 --> 00:22:15,336
This correction is not permission to switch the field fluid. Follow
the current BC plan, consultation and patient-specific reassessment.
Explain the local choice accurately without inventing a universal
lethal mechanism.

91
00:22:15,336 --> 00:22:28,272
Muscle groups lie inside fascia that does not stretch freely. Bleeding
or edema can raise pressure; rising compartment pressure reduces
capillary perfusion and may injure nerve and muscle.

92
00:22:28,272 --> 00:22:40,776
Worsening pain, a tense compartment, pain with passive stretch where
assessed, sensory change or weakness can raise concern. No single
finding rules the condition in or out.

93
00:22:40,776 --> 00:22:52,104
Distal pulses can remain present early because pressure sufficient to
impair capillary flow may be lower than arterial pressure. Waiting for
pulselessness risks late recognition.

94
00:22:52,104 --> 00:23:06,048
A muscle compartment contains tissue, nerves and vessels within a
relatively unyielding fascial boundary. More volume inside raises
pressure and can impair low-pressure venous drainage, making swelling
worse.

95
00:23:06,048 --> 00:23:22,656
The key is loss of the pressure gradient supporting tissue perfusion.
Compartment pressure does not need to exceed systolic blood pressure
before microvascular flow is threatened. A palpable artery farther
down the limb does not demonstrate adequate muscle perfusion.

96
00:23:22,656 --> 00:23:39,792
Increasing pain, pain with passive movement, tense swelling and
sensory or motor changes raise concern. Findings can be incomplete,
especially when consciousness or communication is impaired. No single
sign safely excludes the problem.

97
00:23:39,792 --> 00:23:56,040
Repeat and time-stamp the examination, document analgesia and
response, and seek urgent escalation. Hospital pressure measurement
and surgical decisions are not replaced by a field pulse check or a
fixed number of hours assumed to be safe.

98
00:23:56,040 --> 00:24:09,384
Crush syndrome describes a systemic pathophysiologic risk associated
with significant muscle compression and release. Compartment syndrome
describes pressure-related ischemia in a confined anatomical space.

99
00:24:09,384 --> 00:24:22,680
They may coexist, but one does not prove the other. Crush release can
produce systemic electrolyte and volume problems; a compartment may
threaten a limb even without a dramatic systemic presentation.

100
00:24:22,680 --> 00:24:34,296
Useful handover describes mechanism, duration, changes in pain or
perfusion, interventions and release time. Do not replace serial
findings with a single label.

101
00:24:34,296 --> 00:24:51,000
In this original rehearsal, a person has a thigh pinned beneath
equipment. Before coordinated release the patient is awake, with tense
swelling and a pressure of one hundred eighteen over seventy-four.
That initial pressure does not make release risk disappear.

102
00:24:51,000 --> 00:25:08,952
After release, the pulse is one hundred twenty-four, pressure is
ninety-four over sixty, and the patient is more drowsy. First respond
to the deteriorating patient and reassess the primary survey; do not
spend the transition arguing over one diagnostic label.

103
00:25:08,952 --> 00:25:25,968
Consider volume loss, electrolyte disturbance and acidosis alongside
bleeding, chest injury, hypoxia and other causes. Report the concern
for crush-related deterioration, but do not claim a potassium result
from a monitor waveform alone.

104
00:25:25,968 --> 00:25:42,288
Hand over the estimated compression time and muscle region, exact
release time, serial findings, care before and after release,
consultation and response. That timeline is more useful than saying
only that the patient has crush syndrome.

105
00:25:42,288 --> 00:25:56,448
Capillary leak describes increased movement of fluid and proteins out
of vessels. Hypoxemia describes low arterial oxygen; cyanosis,
confusion or a low saturation can have several causes.

106
00:25:56,448 --> 00:26:06,600
Reperfusion is restored blood flow after ischemia; it can restore
oxygen delivery while also carrying accumulated cellular contents into
circulation.

107
00:26:06,600 --> 00:26:18,744
Third-spacing is a conceptual way to describe fluid that is no longer
effectively available to the circulating volume. It is not a precise
bedside measurement or an independent fluid order.

108
00:26:18,744 --> 00:26:31,824
SIRS means systemic inflammatory response syndrome. Major burns or
other severe injuries can produce systemic inflammation without
infection. A rapid pulse and fever alone do not prove sepsis.

109
00:26:31,824 --> 00:26:47,328
ARDS means acute respiratory distress syndrome. Inflammatory damage to
the lung barrier can cause edema and poor gas exchange. It is not a
name for every low saturation or a diagnosis made from one field
finding.

110
00:26:47,328 --> 00:27:03,216
Hyperkalemia means elevated potassium in blood. That can disturb
cardiac conduction, but an ECG is not a potassium assay. Connect the
crush history to monitoring and urgent support rather than claiming a
laboratory value you have not measured.

111
00:27:03,216 --> 00:27:17,520
Fasciotomy is a surgical opening of fascia to decompress an affected
compartment when indicated. Your learning task is recognition,
reassessment and timely escalation, not attempting a field surgical
release.

112
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:34,080
The Week 9 outline calls descent an absolute first priority before
oxygen or medication, and calls CO-oximetry mandatory for every
enclosed fire. Those statements are retained as school prompts but
should not be taught as universal clinical rules.

113
00:27:34,080 --> 00:27:50,160
Current altitude guidance treats urgent descent and indicated oxygen
as complementary actions. Current BCEHS J02 calls for oxygen and
consultation for suspected carbon monoxide exposure; standard pulse
oximetry may mislead.

114
00:27:50,160 --> 00:28:03,792
For written exams, ask which dated school revision is assessed. For
clinical learning, use current BCEHS guidance and authorized scope; do
not merge conflicting statements into a compromise algorithm.
